<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hakka people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hakka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hakka are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> people whose ancestors migrated in waves from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the hill country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>, keeping a distinct Hakka language. Many emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, where they worked as miners and farmers, notably in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, was Hakka, and Hakka communities supplied many of its first followers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hakka-people.jpg" alt="Hakka people" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KotaKinabalu_Sabah_Gaya-Street-Sunday-Market-17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hakka are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> sub-ethnic group whose settlements are widely dispersed across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and whose language is closely related to Gan, spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>. Their name, written 客家, means guest families. They are distinguished from other southern Han by their dispersal and their tendency to occupy marginal, hilly land. They are commonly held to have come from the Central Plain in the north and to have arrived in the south much later than other southern Han. Earlier settlers had taken the fertile lowlands, assimilated local peoples and driven others, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanka">Tanka</a>, to the coasts and rivers, so the Hakka had to settle poorer territory.</p><p>The Hakka identify as Han, and a 2009 genetic study found them principally of Han descent, with a slight skew towards northern Han compared with other southern groups, despite recorded intermarriage with the Yao and the She. The word Hakka is sometimes also used for other southern Han groups on their southward migration, which complicates the historical record.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People of Hakka ancestry formed the mainstay of the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom, which began at the Hakka village of Jintian in Guiping, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>, whose first followers were Hakka peasants. The Cantonese regarded the Hakka as opportunists intruding on their territory, and friction between the groups sometimes turned violent. The Red Turban rebels, mostly of Cantonese ancestry, carried out a genocidal campaign against the Hakka during their revolt of 1854–1856, killing 13 village chiefs and 7,630 others on the way to Heshan and another 1,320 after taking it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-army">Xiang Army</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> massacred tens of thousands of Hakka in Guanghai, a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Taishan</a>.</p><p>Hakka went overseas in large numbers, chiefly to Malaysia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and also to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The first Hakka reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in the 1880s and Tahiti in 1865. In Taiwan they are the second-largest ethnic group, descended largely from migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> who were the island's first agriculturalists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Poor hill land shaped Hakka culture, and many men turned to military or public careers. Hakka women did not bind their feet and are known for taking on hard work that other groups reserved for men, perhaps from necessity. The Hakka hold Confucian values such as respect for family, ancestor veneration and learning. They built tulou, fortified earth buildings, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, and a representative sample of ten Fujian tulou was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2008. Their cuisine uses preserved meats and tofu and stewed and braised dishes, and their hill songs carry across valleys.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hakka people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government, commanded by Manchus, had grown corrupt and was weak in the south, where anti-Manchu feeling was strongest among the <b>Hakka</b>, a Han Chinese subgroup, and where Christian missionaries were active.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Many <b>Hakka</b> communities in West Kalimantan descend from its miners.</blockquote><a href="/state/heshun-confederation">Heshun Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>Luo Fangbo, a <b>Hakka</b> scholar and migrant from Guangdong, organised Chinese gold miners near Pontianak and in 1777 founded the Lanfang kongsi at Mandor.</blockquote><a href="/state/lanfang-republic">Lanfang Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Hong Xiuquan, a <b>Hakka</b> from Guangdong who repeatedly failed the civil service examinations, came to believe from visions and Christian tracts that he was the younger brother of Jesus.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiping-heavenly-kingdom">Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
